<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Waalo</h1><p class="st-meta">1287 CE – 1855 CE (approx.) · capital Ndiourbel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Wolof kingdom at the mouth of the Senegal river, squeezed for two centuries between Moorish emirates and the French at Saint-Louis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Waalo occupied the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> valley and delta. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> tradition makes it the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndiadiane-ndiaye">Ndiadiane Ndiaye</a>, the legendary founder of the Wolof political order, and the kingdom is dated to the late 13th century. For much of its early history it was a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire</a>, ruled by a king titled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brak">Brak</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brak">Brak</a> was chosen from three royal maternal lineages by a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>, and the queen mother and royal women held real power. After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-jolof">Jolof</a> broke up in 1549 Waalo was independent, trading slaves, gum arabic and grain with the French post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a>, founded in 1659, while suffering raids from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-trarza">Trarza</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> to the north.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1819 or 1820 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-trarza">Trarza</a> and Moorish raiders attacked the royal town of Nder, where the women of the court chose death by fire rather than capture. Under Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndatte-yalla-mbodj">Ndatte Yalla</a> resistance continued, but the French governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Faidherbe</a> annexed Waalo in 1855 and it became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/waalo.jpg" alt="Waalo" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Waalo_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1287 CE – 1855 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ndiourbel</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ndiadiane Ndiaye, Ndatte Yalla Mbodj</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Wolof traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wolof</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Senegal, Mauritania</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Waalo, Walo, Oualo</dd></dl></aside></div>
